## Feature Examples

### Export pipeline (deep module vs shallow pass-through)

```javascript
// BAD — shallow: callers must know file format, paths, and steps
async function readJson(path) { return JSON.parse(await fs.readFile(path)); }
async function writeJson(path, data) { await fs.writeFile(path, JSON.stringify(data)); }
async function validateSchema(data, schema) { /* ... */ }

// GOOD — deep: one concept, one interface, details hidden
class ProjectExporter {
  /** Export a no-code project to a runnable scaffold at destination. */
  async export(sourceProject, destination) {
    const model = await this.#loadAndValidate(sourceProject);
    await this.#materialize(model, destination);
  }
}
```

### Information hiding (don't leak storage shape)

```javascript
// BAD — callers depend on internal representation
class UserStore {
  getRows() { return this.db.query('SELECT * FROM users'); }
  setRows(rows) { this.rows = rows; }
}

// GOOD — callers depend on the concept, not the table
class UserStore {
  async findByEmail(email) { /* ... */ }
  async save(user) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Interface design (eliminate call-order traps)

```javascript
// BAD — fragile temporal API
const parser = new ConfigParser();
parser.setFormat('json');
parser.setStrict(true);
parser.load(file); // throws if order wrong

// GOOD — valid states only; options at construction
const config = await Config.load(file, { strict: true });
```

### Pull complexity downward (don't push flags to every caller)

```javascript
// BAD — every call site repeats edge handling
function renderPage(page, { isMobile, isPreview, locale }) {
  if (isPreview) { /* ... */ }
  if (isMobile) { /* ... */ }
  // ...
}

// GOOD — caller passes intent; module owns the matrix
function renderPage(page, { viewport, mode, locale }) {
  return ViewportRenderer.for(viewport, mode).render(page, locale);
}
```

### Special-general decomposition (isolate edge cases)

```javascript
// BAD — core path cluttered with rare branches
function calculatePrice(cart, user) {
  if (user.isEmployee && cart.hasGiftCard && cart.region === 'EU') { /* ... */ }
  // normal pricing buried below
}

// GOOD — general case stays obvious
function calculatePrice(cart, user) {
  const base = priceEngine.quote(cart);
  return adjustmentRules.apply(base, user, cart);
}
```

### Error handling (define away invalid states)

```javascript
// BAD — half-valid object; callers must tiptoe
const order = createOrder(items); // may lack shipping
order.setShipping(address);
order.submit(); // throws if shipping missing

// GOOD — invalid states unrepresentable
const order = Order.create(items, shippingAddress);
await order.submit();
```

### Combine vs separate (one coherent module)

```javascript
// BAD — readers jump across shallow files for one idea
// validate.js, normalize.js, persist.js — each ~5 lines, no real boundary

// GOOD — separation only when it hides a real design decision
// validation/normalization co-located with the domain type they protect
class ExportManifest {
  static parse(raw) { /* validate + normalize here */ }
}
```

### Naming (abstraction, not mechanism)

```javascript
// BAD
function fetchFromS3AndParseJson(bucket, key) { /* ... */ }

// GOOD
function loadProjectSnapshot(location) { /* ... */ }
```

### Comments (contract and why, not obvious what)

```javascript
// BAD — narrates steps the code already shows
// Loop through pages and write each file
for (const page of pages) {
  await fs.writeFile(path.join(pagesDir, `${page.name}.jsx`), renderPage(page));
}

// BAD — comment replaces a missing name
// Convert route to PascalCase component filename
function r2c(route) { /* ... */ }

// BAD — restates the next line
// Set quotes to true for HTML attributes
escapeFor(href, { quotes: true });

// GOOD — names carry the what; comment carries non-obvious domain context
/** Numeric path segments are Builder route IDs, not page slugs. */
function routeToComponentName(route) { /* ... */ }

// GOOD — documents an invariant or constraint not visible in signatures
/** Destination is wiped before bootstrap; valid only on an empty target dir. */
async function materialize(model, destination) { /* ... */ }

// GOOD — explains a trade-off or external constraint
/** Does not run npm install — user runs that from README after export. */
async function bootstrapViteScaffold(destination) { /* ... */ }
```

When generating or reviewing code, fight complexity directly: deeper modules, simpler interfaces, stronger hiding, fewer special cases.
